1
00:00:00,667 --> 00:00:03,847
Take one alternating supply, and connect three things

2
00:00:03,867 --> 00:00:05,177
to it in turn.

3
00:00:05,307 --> 00:00:06,377
A resistor.

4
00:00:06,427 --> 00:00:07,337
A coil.

5
00:00:07,387 --> 00:00:08,577
A capacitor.

6
00:00:08,747 --> 00:00:12,727
In all three, an alternating voltage produces an alternating

7
00:00:12,747 --> 00:00:13,617
current.

8
00:00:13,787 --> 00:00:16,417
Same frequency, same smooth shape.

9
00:00:16,507 --> 00:00:18,257
So far, nothing surprising.

10
00:00:18,667 --> 00:00:20,097
But look at the timing.

11
00:00:20,427 --> 00:00:24,167
In the resistor, the current rises and falls exactly with

12
00:00:24,187 --> 00:00:24,977
the voltage.

13
00:00:25,307 --> 00:00:27,857
In the coil, the current runs late.

14
00:00:28,187 --> 00:00:30,737
In the capacitor, it runs early.

15
00:00:30,987 --> 00:00:34,337
Last time, we called that gap in timing phase.

16
00:00:34,667 --> 00:00:37,537
In this video we find out where it comes from.

17
00:00:37,947 --> 00:00:41,607
The answer explains why a coil can limit a current without

18
00:00:41,627 --> 00:00:45,207
getting warm, why a capacitor passes a current although

19
00:00:45,227 --> 00:00:48,807
nothing crosses its gap, and why engineers talk about

20
00:00:48,827 --> 00:00:52,087
a second kind of opposition that is not resistance at

21
00:00:52,107 --> 00:00:52,657
all.

22
00:00:54,033 --> 00:00:57,703
Start with the resistor, because it holds no surprises.

23
00:00:58,113 --> 00:01:01,663
A resistor obeys Ohm's law at every instant.

24
00:01:01,793 --> 00:01:04,253
Whatever the voltage is at this moment,

25
00:01:04,273 --> 00:01:07,823
the current is that voltage divided by the resistance.

26
00:01:07,953 --> 00:01:09,933
When the voltage is at its peak,

27
00:01:09,953 --> 00:01:11,223
so is the current.

28
00:01:11,313 --> 00:01:14,103
When the voltage is zero, the current is zero.

29
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:17,863
When the voltage reverses, the current reverses with it.

30
00:01:17,953 --> 00:01:20,183
So the two waves are in phase.

31
00:01:20,353 --> 00:01:21,863
They keep perfect step.

32
00:01:22,273 --> 00:01:23,463
Now look at the power.

33
00:01:23,793 --> 00:01:26,343
Power is voltage times current.

34
00:01:26,433 --> 00:01:29,223
When both are positive, the power is positive.

35
00:01:29,473 --> 00:01:32,813
When both are negative, a negative times a negative is

36
00:01:32,833 --> 00:01:33,783
positive again.

37
00:01:34,033 --> 00:01:36,743
So the power never goes below zero.

38
00:01:36,833 --> 00:01:40,053
A resistor takes energy on both halves of the cycle,

39
00:01:40,073 --> 00:01:42,343
and turns all of it into heat.

40
00:01:42,433 --> 00:01:44,253
The power does pulse.

41
00:01:44,273 --> 00:01:47,373
It swells and fades twice in every cycle.

42
00:01:47,393 --> 00:01:51,613
But its average is simple: the RMS voltage times the RMS

43
00:01:51,633 --> 00:01:52,573
current.

44
00:01:52,593 --> 00:01:56,333
That, you will remember, is exactly what RMS was invented

45
00:01:56,353 --> 00:01:56,983
for.

46
00:01:58,333 --> 00:01:59,523
Now the coil.

47
00:01:59,933 --> 00:02:02,443
Remember what a coil cares about.

48
00:02:02,573 --> 00:02:06,153
In the video on inductance, we saw that a coil does not

49
00:02:06,173 --> 00:02:07,363
oppose a current.

50
00:02:07,613 --> 00:02:10,273
It opposes a change in current.

51
00:02:10,293 --> 00:02:13,593
The voltage across it depends on how fast the current

52
00:02:13,613 --> 00:02:14,793
is changing.

53
00:02:14,813 --> 00:02:19,363
So ask: when is an alternating current changing fastest?

54
00:02:19,693 --> 00:02:21,033
Not at its crest.

55
00:02:21,053 --> 00:02:24,553
At the crest it has stopped rising and has not yet started

56
00:02:24,573 --> 00:02:25,513
to fall.

57
00:02:25,533 --> 00:02:28,473
For an instant it is not changing at all.

58
00:02:28,493 --> 00:02:32,633
So at that instant the voltage across the coil is zero.

59
00:02:32,653 --> 00:02:36,313
The current changes fastest as it sweeps through zero,

60
00:02:36,333 --> 00:02:39,113
on its way from one direction to the other.

61
00:02:39,133 --> 00:02:42,073
So that is when the voltage is greatest.

62
00:02:42,093 --> 00:02:43,913
Put the two side by side.

63
00:02:43,933 --> 00:02:47,593
The voltage peaks, and only a quarter of a cycle later

64
00:02:47,613 --> 00:02:49,113
does the current peak.

65
00:02:49,133 --> 00:02:53,273
In a coil, the current lags the voltage by ninety degrees.

66
00:02:53,293 --> 00:02:54,713
You can feel why.

67
00:02:54,733 --> 00:02:58,393
Think of pushing a heavy trolley back and forth on rails.

68
00:02:58,413 --> 00:03:00,793
You push hardest at the very start,

69
00:03:00,813 --> 00:03:02,393
when it is standing still.

70
00:03:02,413 --> 00:03:05,043
It reaches its greatest speed later,

71
00:03:05,133 --> 00:03:07,033
just as your push dies away.

72
00:03:07,053 --> 00:03:09,993
The trolley's speed lags your push.

73
00:03:10,013 --> 00:03:14,003
Its mass does to motion what inductance does to current.

74
00:03:15,367 --> 00:03:18,707
A coil on alternating current limits the current,

75
00:03:18,727 --> 00:03:20,587
just as a resistor would.

76
00:03:20,607 --> 00:03:23,747
But it does so in a completely different way,

77
00:03:23,767 --> 00:03:25,747
and we give it a different name:

78
00:03:25,767 --> 00:03:27,037
reactance.

79
00:03:27,207 --> 00:03:28,557
How large is it?

80
00:03:28,727 --> 00:03:30,157
Think about the frequency.

81
00:03:30,567 --> 00:03:32,667
The faster the supply alternates,

82
00:03:32,687 --> 00:03:34,867
the faster the current has to change,

83
00:03:34,887 --> 00:03:37,277
and the harder the coil pushes back.

84
00:03:37,447 --> 00:03:40,557
Double the frequency, and the reactance doubles.

85
00:03:40,647 --> 00:03:43,997
And a larger inductance pushes back harder still.

86
00:03:44,167 --> 00:03:47,227
So the reactance of a coil is two pi,

87
00:03:47,247 --> 00:03:50,037
times the frequency, times the inductance.

88
00:03:50,167 --> 00:03:53,507
It is measured in ohms, because it is a voltage divided

89
00:03:53,527 --> 00:03:54,717
by a current.

90
00:03:54,887 --> 00:03:58,067
A coil of a tenth of a henry, at fifty hertz,

91
00:03:58,087 --> 00:04:00,717
has a reactance of about thirty-one ohms.

92
00:04:01,047 --> 00:04:04,387
At five thousand hertz, the same coil has three thousand

93
00:04:04,407 --> 00:04:05,037
ohms.

94
00:04:05,367 --> 00:04:08,147
And for a steady current, at zero frequency,

95
00:04:08,167 --> 00:04:09,357
it has none.

96
00:04:09,527 --> 00:04:13,267
That is why a coil passes direct current freely and holds

97
00:04:13,287 --> 00:04:15,037
back alternating current.

98
00:04:15,207 --> 00:04:17,757
But these are not the ohms of a resistor.

99
00:04:17,847 --> 00:04:20,717
A resistor with thirty-one ohms gets hot.

100
00:04:20,807 --> 00:04:23,917
An ideal coil with thirty-one ohms does not.

101
00:04:24,007 --> 00:04:25,677
We will see why in a moment.

102
00:04:26,167 --> 00:04:29,667
The word reactance was proposed in eighteen ninety-three,

103
00:04:29,687 --> 00:04:32,627
by a French engineer, Édouard Hospitalier,

104
00:04:32,647 --> 00:04:35,757
as alternating current was first being put to work.

105
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:40,850
Now the capacitor, and here everything is the other way

106
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:41,490
round.

107
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:45,530
A capacitor's voltage depends on the charge on its plates.

108
00:04:45,660 --> 00:04:48,370
Current is charge arriving or leaving.

109
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:52,680
So the current is large when the voltage is changing fast,

110
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:55,330
and zero when the voltage is not changing.

111
00:04:55,820 --> 00:04:57,090
Follow one cycle.

112
00:04:57,420 --> 00:05:00,530
Start with the capacitor empty and the supply voltage

113
00:05:00,620 --> 00:05:02,050
just beginning to rise.

114
00:05:02,380 --> 00:05:05,800
The voltage is climbing as steeply as it ever does,

115
00:05:05,820 --> 00:05:08,210
so charge pours onto the plates.

116
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:10,690
The current is at its greatest.

117
00:05:10,860 --> 00:05:13,000
As the voltage nears its crest,

118
00:05:13,020 --> 00:05:14,210
it levels off.

119
00:05:14,300 --> 00:05:17,650
The plates are nearly full, and the flow dwindles.

120
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:21,200
At the crest itself, the voltage stops changing,

121
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:22,690
and the current is zero.

122
00:05:23,180 --> 00:05:25,480
Then the voltage starts to fall,

123
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:27,410
and the charge flows back out.

124
00:05:27,740 --> 00:05:29,880
The current has already reversed,

125
00:05:29,900 --> 00:05:31,970
while the voltage is still positive.

126
00:05:32,460 --> 00:05:35,720
So in a capacitor the current peaks first,

127
00:05:35,740 --> 00:05:38,370
and the voltage a quarter of a cycle later.

128
00:05:38,780 --> 00:05:41,810
The current leads the voltage by ninety degrees.

129
00:05:42,220 --> 00:05:44,290
Think of filling a tank from the bottom.

130
00:05:44,540 --> 00:05:47,810
The water rushes in fastest when the tank is empty.

131
00:05:48,140 --> 00:05:51,000
As the level rises, the flow slows,

132
00:05:51,020 --> 00:05:53,330
and when the tank is full, it stops.

133
00:05:53,660 --> 00:05:55,170
The flow comes first.

134
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:56,690
The level follows.

135
00:05:57,020 --> 00:06:00,520
And notice: no charge ever crosses the gap between the

136
00:06:00,540 --> 00:06:01,410
plates.

137
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:04,840
The current we measure is charge flowing onto one plate

138
00:06:04,860 --> 00:06:07,610
and off the other, back and forth.

139
00:06:07,740 --> 00:06:10,160
As far as the rest of the circuit can tell,

140
00:06:10,180 --> 00:06:12,130
a current is passing through.

141
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:15,650
A capacitor has reactance too.

142
00:06:15,980 --> 00:06:18,290
But it behaves in the opposite way.

143
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:22,040
Raise the frequency, and the plates are charged and discharged

144
00:06:22,060 --> 00:06:24,120
more often every second.

145
00:06:24,140 --> 00:06:27,960
More charge moves each second, so the current is larger.

146
00:06:27,980 --> 00:06:31,640
A larger capacitor holds more charge for the same voltage,

147
00:06:31,660 --> 00:06:34,320
so again the current is larger.

148
00:06:34,340 --> 00:06:37,880
So the reactance of a capacitor goes down as the frequency

149
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:38,920
goes up.

150
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:42,760
It is one, divided by two pi, times the frequency,

151
00:06:42,780 --> 00:06:44,250
times the capacitance.

152
00:06:44,380 --> 00:06:46,840
A capacitor of a hundred microfarads,

153
00:06:46,860 --> 00:06:51,080
at fifty hertz, has a reactance of about thirty-two ohms.

154
00:06:51,100 --> 00:06:54,200
At five thousand hertz, a third of an ohm.

155
00:06:54,220 --> 00:06:57,200
And for a steady voltage, at zero frequency,

156
00:06:57,220 --> 00:06:59,640
its reactance is beyond measure:

157
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:01,090
no current at all.

158
00:07:01,180 --> 00:07:04,840
A capacitor blocks direct current and passes alternating

159
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:05,410
current.

160
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:07,650
Set the two side by side.

161
00:07:07,740 --> 00:07:11,400
As the frequency rises, the coil's reactance climbs and

162
00:07:11,420 --> 00:07:13,010
the capacitor's falls.

163
00:07:13,260 --> 00:07:16,210
At one particular frequency they are equal.

164
00:07:16,380 --> 00:07:18,520
Something remarkable happens there,

165
00:07:18,540 --> 00:07:20,530
and it has a video of its own.

166
00:07:21,900 --> 00:07:23,810
Now we can answer the puzzle.

167
00:07:23,980 --> 00:07:27,240
Why does a coil with thirty-one ohms of reactance stay

168
00:07:27,260 --> 00:07:28,090
cool?

169
00:07:28,300 --> 00:07:32,040
Look at the power again: voltage times current.

170
00:07:32,060 --> 00:07:34,600
In the resistor, the two were in step,

171
00:07:34,620 --> 00:07:37,240
and their product was always positive.

172
00:07:37,260 --> 00:07:40,520
In the coil, they are a quarter of a cycle apart.

173
00:07:40,540 --> 00:07:43,360
For a quarter of the cycle, both are positive,

174
00:07:43,380 --> 00:07:46,040
and the coil takes energy from the supply.

175
00:07:46,060 --> 00:07:49,320
That energy goes into building its magnetic field.

176
00:07:49,340 --> 00:07:53,160
For the next quarter, one is positive and the other negative.

177
00:07:53,180 --> 00:07:54,840
The power is negative.

178
00:07:54,860 --> 00:07:58,360
The field is collapsing, and the coil is handing the energy

179
00:07:58,380 --> 00:07:59,240
back.

180
00:07:59,260 --> 00:08:00,920
Take, give back.

181
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:02,480
Take, give back.

182
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:05,970
Over a whole cycle, the average power is zero.

183
00:08:06,020 --> 00:08:07,920
Nothing is turned into heat.

184
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:11,160
A capacitor does the same, with its electric field.

185
00:08:11,180 --> 00:08:13,320
It borrows energy as it charges,

186
00:08:13,340 --> 00:08:15,640
and returns it as it discharges.

187
00:08:15,660 --> 00:08:18,360
So reactance does not consume energy.

188
00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:20,040
It only borrows it.

189
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:22,600
But notice what that means for the wires.

190
00:08:22,620 --> 00:08:26,280
A real current is flowing back and forth the whole time,

191
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:28,760
carrying energy that nobody uses.

192
00:08:28,780 --> 00:08:31,040
The generator must supply that current,

193
00:08:31,060 --> 00:08:33,160
and the cables must carry it.

194
00:08:33,180 --> 00:08:35,960
This is what engineers call reactive power,

195
00:08:35,980 --> 00:08:39,170
and the cost of it will be the subject of a later video.

196
00:08:40,533 --> 00:08:44,673
Because coils and capacitors treat frequencies so differently,

197
00:08:44,693 --> 00:08:48,163
we can use them to sort one frequency from another.

198
00:08:48,373 --> 00:08:50,723
Look inside a loudspeaker cabinet.

199
00:08:50,853 --> 00:08:54,113
There is a large cone for the low notes and a small one

200
00:08:54,133 --> 00:08:55,483
for the high notes.

201
00:08:55,653 --> 00:08:58,923
A coil is wired in line with the large cone.

202
00:08:59,093 --> 00:09:01,633
Low frequencies pass through it easily,

203
00:09:01,653 --> 00:09:03,723
and high ones are held back.

204
00:09:03,893 --> 00:09:07,153
A capacitor is wired in line with the small cone,

205
00:09:07,173 --> 00:09:08,673
and does the opposite.

206
00:09:08,693 --> 00:09:11,793
Between them, they send each note to the cone that can

207
00:09:11,813 --> 00:09:12,913
play it.

208
00:09:12,933 --> 00:09:16,433
A coil is also the gentlest way to limit an alternating

209
00:09:16,453 --> 00:09:17,353
current.

210
00:09:17,373 --> 00:09:20,913
A resistor would do it by wasting energy as heat.

211
00:09:20,933 --> 00:09:25,153
A coil does it by reactance, and wastes almost nothing.

212
00:09:25,173 --> 00:09:28,643
That is the job of the choke in an old fluorescent lamp.

213
00:09:28,773 --> 00:09:31,873
And here is a way to remember which way round the timing

214
00:09:31,893 --> 00:09:32,443
goes.

215
00:09:32,853 --> 00:09:34,523
Think of the word CIVIL.

216
00:09:34,853 --> 00:09:37,713
In a capacitor, C, the current,

217
00:09:37,733 --> 00:09:40,443
I, comes before the voltage, V.

218
00:09:40,773 --> 00:09:43,123
That is C, I, V.

219
00:09:43,253 --> 00:09:46,033
In an inductor, L, the voltage,

220
00:09:46,053 --> 00:09:48,603
V, comes before the current, I.

221
00:09:48,933 --> 00:09:52,043
Read from the middle: V, I, L.

222
00:09:52,133 --> 00:09:54,403
Capacitor, current leads.

223
00:09:54,453 --> 00:09:56,923
Inductor, current lags.

224
00:09:58,267 --> 00:09:59,977
So let's put it together.

225
00:10:00,107 --> 00:10:03,647
In a resistor, the current keeps step with the voltage,

226
00:10:03,667 --> 00:10:06,137
and all the energy becomes heat.

227
00:10:06,267 --> 00:10:10,007
In a coil, the voltage depends on how fast the current

228
00:10:10,027 --> 00:10:11,057
is changing.

229
00:10:11,147 --> 00:10:14,817
So the current lags the voltage by a quarter of a cycle.

230
00:10:15,147 --> 00:10:18,887
In a capacitor, the current depends on how fast the voltage

231
00:10:18,907 --> 00:10:19,697
is changing.

232
00:10:19,947 --> 00:10:23,097
So the current leads by a quarter of a cycle.

233
00:10:23,227 --> 00:10:26,327
Both oppose alternating current with reactance,

234
00:10:26,347 --> 00:10:27,777
measured in ohms.

235
00:10:27,867 --> 00:10:30,777
A coil's reactance rises with frequency.

236
00:10:30,827 --> 00:10:32,737
A capacitor's falls.

237
00:10:32,827 --> 00:10:35,217
And reactance consumes no energy.

238
00:10:35,387 --> 00:10:37,657
It borrows it, and gives it back.

239
00:10:37,787 --> 00:10:40,617
But real circuits are never just one of these.

240
00:10:40,667 --> 00:10:44,247
They have resistance, inductance and capacitance all at

241
00:10:44,267 --> 00:10:48,327
once, and the current is neither in step nor a full quarter

242
00:10:48,347 --> 00:10:49,297
cycle out.

243
00:10:49,627 --> 00:10:52,817
To deal with that, an engineer in eighteen ninety-three

244
00:10:52,907 --> 00:10:56,487
found a way to replace every wave with a simple turning

245
00:10:56,507 --> 00:10:56,977
arrow.

246
00:10:57,467 --> 00:11:00,617
Next time: phasors, and impedance.

247
00:11:00,747 --> 00:11:04,167
If this helped you see it, and not just calculate it,

248
00:11:04,187 --> 00:11:07,217
please like, share, and subscribe to this channel.
